## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to respond to production incidents with a concrete, beginner-friendly plan focusing on observable signals, containment, rollback, and validation under time pressure.

## Key Concepts
- Observability: latency p95, error rate, queue depth, redis/backlog metrics
- Containment: throttle concurrency, pause new work, revert patch safely
- Rollback & Validation: restore previous config, synthetic tests, end-to-end checks

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple end-to-end validation stub
async function checkDelivery(queueClient, producer) {
  const msg = {id: 'test', payload: 'ping'};
  await producer.send(msg);
  const ok = await queueClient.awaitDeliveryAck(msg.id, 5000);
  return ok;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you automate the rollback trigger and rollback-on-fail thresholds?
- Which metrics would you add to distinguish transient spikes from persistent issues?

## Why This Is Asked
This probes cross-region real-time reliability, backpressure handling, and semantics trade-offs in a live data plane.

## Key Concepts
- Tail latency monitoring; regional failover
- Backpressure and traffic shaping
- Exactly-once vs at-least-once semantics
- Offset management and idempotent processing
- Chaos testing and post-incident reviews

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo idempotent replay handler
function processEvent(evt){
  const id = evt.id;
  if (processedSet.has(id)) return;
  store.update(evt.payload);
  processedSet.add(id);
  commitOffsets(evt.offset);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate this plan with simulated regional outages?
- What telemetry would you add to quantify readiness and bottlenecks?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design automated degradation with controlled risk during external dependency pressure and multi-region operation.

## Key Concepts
- Circuit breakers and failover
- Per-region degradation strategies
- Idempotent queues and exactly-once delivery
- Canary and synthetic testing
- Rollback criteria and observability

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code for circuit breaker state machine
class CircuitBreaker {
  constructor(threshold, resetTime) {
    this.threshold = threshold;
    this.resetTime = resetTime;
    this.failures = 0;
    this.state = 'CLOSED';
  }
  onFailure() {
    this.failures++;
    if (this.failures > this.threshold) this.state = 'OPEN';
  }
  canRequest() { return this.state !== 'OPEN'; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for multi-tenant services with mixed SLIs?
- How would you validate degradation paths in canaries without impacting customers?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical mastery of deduplication in real systems, combining transactional patterns, Kafka exactly-once semantics, and robust auditing.

## Key Concepts

- Exactly-once processing
- Transactional outbox pattern
- Kafka transactions (transactional.id)
- Idempotency keys and dedupe stores
- Compensating actions and audit trails

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: within a DB transaction
BEGIN;
UPDATE orders SET status='PAID' WHERE id = :orderId;
INSERT INTO outbox (order_id, event_type, payload, created_at) VALUES (:orderId, 'OrderPaid', :payload, NOW());
COMMIT;
```

